        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 741,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 706,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the logistic sigmoid, 1 / (1 + e to the minus x), to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt squeezes any score into a probability between 0 and 1 - the last step of a classifier, or a smooth switch that is off for very negative input and on for very positive input.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from 0 to 1, passing 0.5 at x = 0. It is never quite 0 or 1:\nexpit(4) = 0.982, expit(8) = 0.99966. expit(-x) = 1 - expit(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.expit\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number and always strictly between 0 and 1 (in 32-bit floats it reaches exactly 1 near x = 17).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-8 to 8 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is expit of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -8 to 8, drawn from -0.05 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid gives exactly the same numbers (torch implements expit as the sigmoid) - it is the same curve under its neural-network name. t.special.logits undoes it. t.nn.tanh is the same S stretched to -1..1, and t.special.ndtr is a similar S with slightly thinner tails.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the logistic sigmoid, 1 / (1 + e to the minus x), to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt squeezes any score into a probability between 0 and 1 - the last step of a classifier, or a smooth switch that is off for very negative input and on for very positive input.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from 0 to 1, passing 0.5 at x = 0. It is never quite 0 or 1:\nexpit(4) = 0.982, expit(8) = 0.99966. expit(-x) = 1 - expit(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.expit\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number and always strictly between 0 and 1 (in 32-bit floats it reaches exactly 1 near x = 17).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-8 to 8 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is expit of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -8 to 8, drawn from -0.05 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid gives exactly the same numbers (torch implements expit as the sigmoid) - it is the same curve under its neural-network name. t.special.logits undoes it. t.nn.tanh is the same S stretched to -1..1, and t.special.ndtr is a similar S with slightly thinner tails."
        },
